<description>
Complete sparse nested rectangular color layers into solid rectangles.

Input structure
The background is the dominant color, and every foreground color marks one
axis-aligned rectangular layer; the layer bboxes have unique areas and are
nested from outermost to innermost.
Rule
Take each color's bbox as its completed rectangle, except that a child bbox
which already matches at least three sides of its parent's one-pixel inset
expands to that full inset; repaint the rectangles largest to smallest.
Key insight
The scattered pixels are samples of nested rectangular regions, so the
output is a clean redraw of those regions with inner colors overwriting
outer colors.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "symmetry/pattern",
  "src": "sparse foreground pixels marking nested axis-aligned rectangular color regions",
  "dst": "same canvas with each nested rectangular region completed",
  "transform": "infer each color's rectangular layer from its bbox; if a child bbox aligns with its parent's one-pixel-inset interior on at least three sides, expand it to that interior; fill layers from largest to smallest so inner colors overwrite outer colors"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: even square, 10-12 rows/cols [observed]
  background: 0 (most frequent color)
  layers: 2-3 distinct non-bg colors, each defining one concentric square ring
  output_structure:
    nested_squares: all layers are concentric axis-aligned filled squares, centered on grid center
    dimensions: all square side lengths are even; sizes strictly decreasing outer to inner
    gaps: uniform per pair (all 4 sides equal); gap is 1 or 2
    painting_order: largest to smallest; inner colors overwrite outer
    outermost: spans full grid or is inset by 1 [observed]; bg fills remaining border if inset
    innermost: solid filled square, side length 4-6 [observed]
  input_structure:
    sparse_sampling: each ring cell is either its correct color or bg; no wrong-color fg pixels
    sampling_rate: 28-71% of each ring's area carries the correct color
    bbox_preservation: input bbox of each color equals output bbox, EXCEPT when gap=1
    side_match_rule: |
      For gap=1 transitions: child input bbox matches parent's 1px inset on 3-4 sides
        (sampling may omit one entire border row/col -> 3 matches -> solver expands to inset)
      For gap>=2 transitions: child input bbox equals output bbox (0 side matches with parent inset)
      Solver asserts side_matches in {0, 3, 4}
  colors: all fg colors are distinct non-zero values; bg is always 0 [observed]

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first, then sparsify
  steps:
    1. Pick grid size N (even, 10-12). Pick 2-3 distinct non-bg colors.
    2. Build concentric squares from outside in. Outermost = NxN (or N-2 x N-2 centered).
       For each successive layer, pick gap g in {1, 2}. Inner square side = outer side - 2*g.
       Ensure innermost side >= 4.
    3. Paint output: fill squares largest to smallest.
    4. Derive input by sparsifying each ring:
       - For gap=1 rings: keep pixels on 3-4 border sides of the child rect (to ensure
         side_matches >= 3 with parent inset). Randomly drop ~30-60% of ring cells to bg.
       - For gap>=2 rings: keep at least one pixel on all 4 border rows/cols of the child rect
         (to preserve input bbox = output bbox). Randomly drop ~30-60% of ring cells to bg.
       - Ensure no cell is assigned a wrong fg color (only correct color or bg).
```
</generation_spec>
